00:55:43,760 --> 00:55:49,680
medical imaging anywhere we want to pull

1376
00:55:46,400 --> 00:55:51,520
a weak signal out of noisy data.

1377
00:55:49,680 --> 00:55:55,400
Crossorrelation gives us a way to do

1378
00:55:51,520 --> 00:55:55,400
that precisely and efficiently.

1379
00:55:57,119 --> 00:56:01,280
Let's take a look at how convolution

1380
00:55:59,119 --> 00:56:03,440
helps with feature extraction,

1381
00:56:01,280 --> 00:56:05,520
especially edge detection, which is

1382
00:56:03,440 --> 00:56:07,680
fundamental in medical imaging and

1383
00:56:05,520 --> 00:56:09,760
computer vision.

1384
00:56:07,680 --> 00:56:12,000
In this example, we're applying a soable

1385
00:56:09,760 --> 00:56:15,440
filter often used to detect edges in the

1386
00:56:12,000 --> 00:56:17,200
horizontal direction.

1387
00:56:15,440 --> 00:56:19,599
We begin with the source image

1388
00:56:17,200 --> 00:56:21,359
represented as a grid of pixel intensity

1389
00:56:19,599 --> 00:56:23,920
values.

1390
00:56:21,359 --> 00:56:27,200
The soil kernel shown here as a 3x3

1391
00:56:23,920 --> 00:56:29,440
matrix contains weights negative - 1 0

1392
00:56:27,200 --> 00:56:32,720
and positive 1 across columns designed

1393
00:56:29,440 --> 00:56:35,280
to emphasize horizontal changes.

1394
00:56:32,720 --> 00:56:38,319
Here's what happens. We align the filter

1395
00:56:35,280 --> 00:56:40,079
over a 3x3 region of the input image.

1396
00:56:38,319 --> 00:56:41,599
Then we compute the sum of products

1397
00:56:40,079 --> 00:56:45,440
between the kernel weights and the

1398
00:56:41,599 --> 00:56:46,880
underlying pixel values.

1399
00:56:45,440 --> 00:56:49,760
For instance, at this highlighted

1400
00:56:46,880 --> 00:56:56,240
region, the computation looks like this.

1401
00:56:49,760 --> 00:57:02,240
1 * 3 + 0 * 0 + 1 * 1 + 2 * 2 + 0 * 6 +

1402
00:56:56,240 --> 00:57:04,799
2 * 2 + 1 * 2 + 0 * 4 + 1 * 1. When we

1403
00:57:02,240 --> 00:57:06,720
add those up, we get three. That value

1404
00:57:04,799 --> 00:57:10,079
becomes the output for the corresponding

1405
00:57:06,720 --> 00:57:11,920
destination pixel.

1406
00:57:10,079 --> 00:57:14,240
Now, as the filter slides across the

1407
00:57:11,920 --> 00:57:16,000
image pixel by pixel, this process

1408
00:57:14,240 --> 00:57:17,839
repeats.

1409
00:57:16,000 --> 00:57:20,000
Wherever there's a strong horizontal

1410
00:57:17,839 --> 00:57:21,920
transition, the filter produces a large

1411
00:57:20,000 --> 00:57:23,520
response.

1412
00:57:21,920 --> 00:57:26,319
Where the region is uniform, the

1413
00:57:23,520 --> 00:57:29,280
response is small or zero.

1414
00:57:26,319 --> 00:57:31,280
This is how edge detection works.

1415
00:57:29,280 --> 00:57:33,359
It uses convolution to detect where

1416
00:57:31,280 --> 00:57:35,359
intensity changes rapidly, allowing us

1417
00:57:33,359 --> 00:57:38,760
to extract important structural features

1418
00:57:35,359 --> 00:57:38,760
from an image.

1419
00:57:40,079 --> 00:57:43,760
To detect edges effectively across all

1420
00:57:42,319 --> 00:57:46,079
directions, we apply multiple

1421
00:57:43,760 --> 00:57:47,839
convolution filters, each oriented

1422
00:57:46,079 --> 00:57:49,839
differently.

1423
00:57:47,839 --> 00:57:52,079
The image on the left shows the original

1424
00:57:49,839 --> 00:57:54,000
grayscale input. On the right, we see

1425
00:57:52,079 --> 00:57:55,839
the result of applying the canny edge

1426
00:57:54,000 --> 00:57:58,720
detector, one of the most widely used

1427
00:57:55,839 --> 00:58:00,559
edge detection algorithms.

1428
00:57:58,720 --> 00:58:03,359
What makes the Canny method powerful is

1429
00:58:00,559 --> 00:58:05,520
its multi-stage approach. It starts with

1430
00:58:03,359 --> 00:58:07,920
smoothing the image to suppress noise,

1431
00:58:05,520 --> 00:58:10,400
then applies gradient-based filters in

1432
00:58:07,920 --> 00:58:12,079
horizontal and vertical directions. From

1433
00:58:10,400 --> 00:58:16,319
there, it calculates the gradient

1434
00:58:12,079 --> 00:58:18,559
magnitude and direction at each pixel.

1435
00:58:16,319 --> 00:58:20,559
The algorithm then performs non-maximum

1436
00:58:18,559 --> 00:58:22,400
suppression to thin out the edges and

1437
00:58:20,559 --> 00:58:25,200
finally applies double thresholding and

1438
00:58:22,400 --> 00:58:28,480
edge tracking by hysteresus to preserve

1439
00:58:25,200 --> 00:58:30,720
only the most significant contours.

1440
00:58:28,480 --> 00:58:32,720
The result is a crisp binary edge map

1441
00:58:30,720 --> 00:58:37,400
like the one shown here which highlights

1442
00:58:32,720 --> 00:58:37,400
boundaries of objects and fine details.

1443
00:58:39,040 --> 00:58:44,880
To sum up, we explored linear systems,

1444
00:58:41,839 --> 00:58:47,119
especially shift and variant systems.

1445
00:58:44,880 --> 00:58:49,040
We saw how convolution helps compute the

1446
00:58:47,119 --> 00:58:51,200
system output.

1447
00:58:49,040 --> 00:58:52,799
We saw how crossorrelation relates to

1448
00:58:51,200 --> 00:58:54,559
convolution and supports feature

1449
00:58:52,799 --> 00:58:56,559
detection.

1450
00:58:54,559 --> 00:58:58,480
We learned how decomposing functions

1451
00:58:56,559 --> 00:59:00,480
into impulses connects to these

1452
00:58:58,480 --> 00:59:02,319
operations.

1453
00:59:00,480 --> 00:59:04,400
Convolution can feel tricky at first,

1454
00:59:02,319 --> 00:59:07,200
but with practice, it becomes a powerful

1455
00:59:04,400 --> 00:59:10,599
intuitive tool.

